.TH HOMELAB-CLI-ADMIN 8 "2026-08-21" "homelab-cli" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
homelab-cli\-admin \- site administration commands for homelab\-cli
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B homelab-cli
.RB [ --api-url
.IR url ]
.B admin
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B homelab-cli admin
groups role, permission, and user-management operations under one
command family. Every subcommand requires the logged-in account to
hold the
.I site_admin
role server-side; the CLI does not enforce this locally, the server
does.
.P
Run
.RI "\(lqhomelab-cli admin " command " --help\(rq"
for a given subcommand's own arguments.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR list-roles ", " role-permissions
List all roles, or the endpoints granted to a specific role.
.TP
.BR grant-permission ", " revoke-permission
Grant or revoke an endpoint (e.g.
.RI \(lqGET\ /api/v1/drive/quota\(rq )
on a role.
.TP
.BR user-roles ", " grant-role ", " revoke-role
List, assign, or revoke a role on a specific user account.
.TP
.B reset-password
Reset a user's password to a randomly generated dictionary passphrase.
Never accepts a client-supplied password \- the new passphrase is
generated server-side and printed exactly once in the response. It is
not recoverable afterward; if lost, reset again.
.TP
.BR revoke-tokens ", " force-relogin
Revoke all of a user's refresh tokens, forcing re-login on their next
token refresh. Both commands perform the identical action against two
distinct server endpoints of the same name \- pick whichever name reads
better in context.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR homelab-cli (8),
.BR homelab-cli-mail (8),
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8)
